---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const WhatCounts = `A COMPUTING INNOVATION

  includes a program as an integral part
  of its function

  PHYSICAL              a self-driving car
  NONPHYSICAL SOFTWARE  photo editing software
  NONPHYSICAL CONCEPT   e-commerce

A hammer is not one. A spreadsheet is.`;

const BothAtOnce = `GPS IN A CAR

  BENEFICIAL   it predicts your arrival time by
               tracking your speed and distance

  HARMFUL      the police obtain that data and
               issue a speeding ticket

Same innovation. Same feature. Same data.`;

const TheSkill = `SKILL 5.C -- DESCRIBE THE IMPACT OF A
COMPUTING INNOVATION

  Identification requires BOTH

    the classification    beneficial or harmful
    the justification     and why, and for whom

"It's bad" is not an answer.
"It's harmful to X because Y" is.`;

const Unintended = `NOT EVERY EFFECT IS ANTICIPATED

  targeted advertising      helps businesses
                            misused at individual
                            and aggregate levels

  machine learning and      enabled medicine,
  data mining               business, science
                            also used to discriminate
                            against groups

Both rows are the exam's own examples.`;

const TwoTruths = `TWO STATEMENTS THAT MUST BE HELD TOGETHER

  "Responsible programmers TRY to consider the
   unintended ways their innovations can be used."

  "It is NOT POSSIBLE for a programmer to consider
   all the ways a computing innovation can be used."

The second is not an excuse for skipping the first.`;

const Scale = `RAPID SHARING

  "Rapid sharing of a program, or running a program
   with a large number of users, can result in
   significant impacts beyond the intended purpose
   or control of the programmer."

Ten users and ten million users are different
programs, even when the code is identical.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 3.11: Beneficial and Harmful Effects</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Largest Thing on the Exam</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Reasoning about computing innovations is 28 to 33% of the
            multiple-choice section</b> &mdash; more than any other skill, including
            writing code. The next eight sessions are that skill.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">And it is not an opinion exercise.</b> There is a
            right way to answer these questions and a wrong way, and the difference is
            whether you say <b>who</b> an effect lands on and <b>why</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 3.11</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Beneficial and Harmful Effects</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>What Counts as an Innovation</p>
              <p>Both, At the Same Time</p>
              <p>Classification Plus Justification</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What Counts</h2>
              <Code code={WhatCounts} frame="code" lang="txt" title="A Computing Innovation" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">The test: <b>is a program an integral part of how it works?</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>People Make Them</h2>
              <p>The exam's first statement on the topic: <b>people create computing innovations.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Which sounds obvious and is doing work.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Nothing here happened <i>to</i> anybody. <b>Somebody chose it.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Central Fact</h2>
              <Code code={BothAtOnce} frame="code" lang="txt" title="One Effect, Two Readings" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">
                <b>A single effect can be beneficial and harmful</b> &mdash; to
                different people, or to the same person.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Not Two Effects. One.</h2>
              <p>The GPS is not doing a good thing and also a bad thing.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>It is doing one thing: recording where you were and how fast.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Whether that is good depends entirely on who is holding the data.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>So How Do You Answer?</h2>
              <Code code={TheSkill} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Both Halves" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Sentence That Scores</h2>
              <p><b>&quot;This is harmful to <i>who</i> because <i>why</i>.&quot;</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Name a person or a group. Name the mechanism.</p>
              <p class="fragment">&quot;It invades privacy&quot; names neither.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Nobody Saw It Coming</h2>
              <Code code={Unintended} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Exam's Own Examples" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Things Both True</h2>
              <Code code={TwoTruths} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Hold Both" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">You are responsible for trying. You will still miss things.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And Scale Changes Everything</h2>
              <Code code={Scale} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Beyond the Programmer's Control" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Your Create Task had one user. That was the easy version.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>It Cuts the Other Way Too</h2>
              <p>Unintended effects are not only harmful.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Advances in computing have <b>increased creativity in other
                fields</b> &mdash; medicine, engineering, communications, the arts.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">Mostly by accident.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And Tasks Change Shape</h2>
              <p><b>&quot;The way people complete tasks often changes to incorporate new computing innovations.&quot;</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Not just faster. <b>Different.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Nobody writes letters faster. They stopped writing letters.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Deciding whether something is a computing innovation</li>
                <li>Explaining how one effect can be both beneficial and harmful</li>
                <li>Answering with a classification <b>and</b> a justification</li>
                <li>Describing effects beyond an innovation's intended purpose</li>
                <li>Explaining why scale produces effects the programmer cannot control</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Computing innovation</dt>
                  <dd>Something that includes a program as an integral part of its function. Physical, nonphysical software, or a nonphysical concept.</dd>
                  <dt>Effect</dt>
                  <dd>An impact, a result, or an outcome. Beneficial and harmful effects are contextual and interpretive.</dd>
                  <dt>Identification</dt>
                  <dd>Both the classification of an effect and the justification for that classification.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Today's Work</h2>

          <p><b>On paper. Full sentences everywhere &mdash; this is a writing session.</b></p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Is It a Computing Innovation?</h3>

          <p><b>Yes or no, and if yes, which of the three kinds.</b></p>

          <ol>
            <li>A hammer</li>
            <li>A smart doorbell</li>
            <li>Online shopping</li>
            <li>A spreadsheet program</li>
            <li>A paper map</li>
            <li>A self-driving car</li>
            <li>A digital thermometer that shows a number and stores nothing</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>Item 7 is deliberately hard.</b> Argue it either way and say what your
            answer depends on.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: One Effect, Two Readings</h3>

          <p>
            For each innovation, <b>name a single feature</b> &mdash; not two &mdash;
            and describe how that one feature is beneficial to somebody and harmful to
            somebody.
          </p>

          <table class="table table-sm align-middle mt-3">
            <thead><tr><th>Innovation</th><th>The one feature</th><th>Beneficial to whom, why</th><th>Harmful to whom, why</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>A school app that shows parents live attendance</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Automatic photo tagging of faces</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>A delivery app that ranks drivers by speed</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The hard rule: it has to be the same feature in
            both columns.</b> If your harm comes from a different feature than your
            benefit, you have described two effects and answered a different question.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Fix the Answers</h3>

          <p>Each of these would score zero. <b>Rewrite it so it would score.</b></p>

          <ol>
            <li>&quot;Social media is bad for people.&quot;</li>
            <li>&quot;Online shopping is convenient.&quot;</li>
            <li>&quot;Facial recognition invades privacy.&quot;</li>
            <li>&quot;Self-driving cars are dangerous.&quot;</li>
            <li>&quot;Streaming music helps artists reach fans.&quot;</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>Each rewrite must name who and why.</b> Two of these five are{" "}
            <i>positive</i> claims that score zero for the same reason as the negative
            ones &mdash; say which, and why that is the same problem.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: Beyond the Intended Purpose</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Pick one innovation</b> from Task 2, or one of your own.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li><b>What was it built for?</b> One sentence.</li>
            <li><b>Describe a use its creators did not intend</b> that is beneficial.</li>
            <li><b>Describe one that is harmful.</b></li>
            <li>
              <b>Could the programmers have anticipated either?</b> Answer using both
              of the exam's statements: that responsible programmers try, and that
              considering every use is not possible.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Now change the scale.</b> Ten users, then ten million. What becomes
              possible at ten million that was not possible at ten?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>What is the test for whether something is a computing innovation?</li>
            <li>What <b>two</b> things does identifying an effect require?</li>
            <li>Can a single effect be both beneficial and harmful? To whom?</li>
            <li>Is a programmer expected to anticipate every use of their program?</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>&quot;People create computing innovations.&quot; Explain why the exam bothers to say something so obvious.</li>
            <li>You wrote a program in Quarter 3 with one user. Describe one harmful effect it could have had if ten million people used it.</li>
            <li>Task 3 had two positive statements that score zero. Explain why a compliment is as unhelpful as an insult on this kind of question.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>All four tasks in full sentences, plus your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
